% ============================================================================
% vertebrate-groups — the five vertebrate CLASSES and the ONE distinctive
% body-covering / characteristic each source assigns to it
% (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% The backboned animals — the vertebrates — fall into five big groups, called
% CLASSES: fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals. The simplest way a
% child (or a biology student) tells them apart is by looking at what covers or
% distinguishes the animal's body: birds have FEATHERS, mammals have HAIR,
% reptiles have DRY SCALY SKIN, amphibians have MOIST THIN SKIN, and fish breathe
% through GILLS. This tiny library records, for each of the five classes, the ONE
% distinctive feature its source states for it. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? vertebrate_trait(bird, $Trait)       % feathers
%     ? vertebrate_trait(reptile, $Trait)    % dry_scaly_skin
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `vertebrate_trait(class, trait)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the trait
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word that
% is not one of these five vertebrate classes (ask it about an `insect` — an
% INVERTEBRATE, not a vertebrate class — and it stays honestly silent rather than
% inventing a trait).
%
% The five vertebrate classes and the distinctive feature the NPS source names
% for each, as a truth table:
%
%     class       distinctive feature   a beginner's cue
%     ---------   -------------------   -----------------------------------------
%     fish        gills                 breathes through gills (not lungs)
%     amphibian   moist_thin_skin       soft, damp skin — part water, part land
%     reptile     dry_scaly_skin        dry, scaly skin and clawed toes
%     bird        feathers              the only animals with feathers
%     mammal      hair                  has hair / fur and feeds its young milk
%
% All five classes appear, so a learner sees the whole vertebrate family. Notice
% the deliberate choice of feature per class: the NPS source lists SEVERAL traits
% for each class (fish are "ectothermic, scales, gills, fins…"), and both fish
% and reptiles are described with scales. If we recalled "scales" for fish it
% would COLLIDE with the reptile row — the same word for two classes — so it
% would no longer distinguish them. Instead each row uses the ONE feature that is
% CLEANLY DISTINCT for its class in this source: fish get GILLS (the respiratory
% feature no other class here has — reptiles, birds and mammals all use lungs),
% amphibians get their MOIST THIN SKIN, reptiles their DRY SCALY SKIN, birds their
% FEATHERS and mammals their HAIR. Five classes, five different values — a shared
% trait is never forced onto a class.
%
% A word that is NOT one of these five vertebrate classes — `insect`, `rock`,
% `bacteria` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a trait recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than fabricating an answer. (An insect is an INVERTEBRATE — it has no backbone —
% so it is not a class of vertebrate at all.) That honest-abstention property is
% what makes a recall table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% class→trait pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `trait` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase snake_case atom, chosen to
% be the source's OWN word(s) for that class's distinctive feature — never one the
% source does not support. (Where the source uses several words — "moist thin
% skin", "dry scaly skin" — the atom keeps the source's own words joined with
% underscores, so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.)
%
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every class→trait pair below
% was read out of a fetched U.S. NATIONAL PARK SERVICE teaching page, and any pair
% whose feature could not be verified there would have been dropped). The page is
% the NPS "Vertebrate Grab Bag" lesson plan (nps.gov), a primary U.S. government
% teaching resource — hence `trust authoritative`. Each pair, with the verbatim
% span of that page's Background section that states it:
%
%   fish → gills
%     "Fish: backbone, ectothermic, scales, gills, fins, jelly-like eggs"
%     (gills is the distinctive respiratory feature — no other class here has it)
%
%   amphibian → moist_thin_skin
%     "Amphibians: backbone, ectothermic, moist thin skin, lungs, no claws,
%      jelly-like eggs"
%
%   reptile → dry_scaly_skin
%     "Reptiles: backbone, ectothermic, dry scaly skin, clawed toes, leathery
%      eggs, lungs"
%
%   bird → feathers
%     "Birds: backbone, endothermic, feathers, hollow bones, hard-shelled eggs,
%      lungs"
%
%   mammal → hair
%     "Mammals: backbone, endothermic, hair / fur, diaphragm, lungs, produces
%      milk"
%
% (The five-class framing itself is stated on the same page:
% "This activity centers on the five major vertebrate classes: fish, amphibian,
% reptile, bird, and mammal.")
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the NPS statement that fixes the
% first row (fish → gills). Every OTHER row's verbatim span is listed above, so
% each trait remains independently checkable. The source is the NPS "Vertebrate
% Grab Bag" lesson plan, a primary U.S. government (National Park Service)
% teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table vertebrate_trait {
    columns class, trait

    row (fish, gills)
    row (amphibian, moist_thin_skin)
    row (reptile, dry_scaly_skin)
    row (bird, feathers)
    row (mammal, hair)

    source "Fish: backbone, ectothermic, scales, gills, fins, jelly-like eggs"
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/teachers/classrooms/vertebrate-grab-bag.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
